A fresh wallet is created from `ctx.dashd.wallet.mnemonic` and passed to `create_and_start_client`, but the receive address comes from `ctx.receive_address()` — a method on `TestContext` that presumably advances its own wallet state, not the fresh `wallet` Arc. These are separate instances. If they have diverged in derivation-index state (e.g. `ctx` pre-populated addresses during `TestContext::new`), the fresh wallet may not track `receive_address`, so the client never observes the transaction. Unlike `test_reorg_with_descendant_tx_chain_emits_wallet_reorg`, there is no `assert!(ctx.has_transaction(&txid).await)` guard before the fork. If `WalletEvent::Reorg` is conditional on wallet-relevant txs in the reorged range, the test will silently hang in `tokio::join!` for the full 60-second timeout rather than giving a clear assertion failure. Add `assert!(ctx.has_transaction(&txid).await)` after `wait_for_sync(original_tip)` as a precondition, mirroring the pattern in the descendant-tx test.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// after wait_for_sync(original_tip)
let txid = ctx.dashd.node.send_to_address(...);
// ...
wait_for_sync(&mut client.progress_receiver, original_tip).await;
assert!(ctx.has_transaction(&txid).await, "wallet must observe tx before reorg");
```
</details>

In `drive_reorg_rebroadcast_at`, orphaned events are sent on `self.wallet_event_tx`, which is a private broadcast channel created inside `MempoolManager::new()`. In `lifecycle.rs`, `set_wallet_event_subscription` only wires the *receive* side of the wallet's own channel into `wallet_event_rx`; nothing in the production path subscribes to `wallet_event_tx`. The `let _ = self.wallet_event_tx.send(...)` call silently discards the event when there are no receivers (broadcast::Sender::send returns Err when there are 0 subscribers). The PR description says this event should surface in the UI for user intervention, but it never reaches EventHandlers or FFI callbacks in production. The fix is to forward `TxRepeatedlyOrphaned` through the wallet's own broadcast sender (requires a `send_wallet_event` method on `WalletInterface`) or thread the orphan sender through to the `DashSpvClient` event dispatch path; the current test-only `#[cfg(test)] pub(super) fn wallet_event_sender` exposes the gap.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// In WalletInterface trait, add:
async fn emit_event(&self, event: WalletEvent);

// In drive_reorg_rebroadcast_at, replace:
let _ = self.wallet_event_tx.send(WalletEvent::TxRepeatedlyOrphaned { ... });
// with:
self.wallet.read().await.emit_event(WalletEvent::TxRepeatedlyOrphaned { ... }).await;
// Caveats: requires trait change and implementation in all WalletInterface implementors.
```
</details>

`locktime_satisfied` checks `lock_time > 0` and then evaluates block-height or timestamp locktime, but it never inspects whether all inputs have `sequence = 0xffffffff`. Per BIP-65 and Dash consensus rules, a transaction is always final if every input's `nSequence == 0xFFFFFFFF`, regardless of `nLockTime`. A demoted transaction may have `lock_time = N` with all inputs at `sequence = 0xffffffff`, making it immediately spendable at any block height. The current code would defer its rebroadcast until the chain tip reaches `N`, causing unnecessary delay and potentially keeping user funds in limbo for many blocks. The fix is to check all input sequences before evaluating lock_time.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
fn locktime_satisfied(&self, tip_height: u32, now: SystemTime) -> bool {
    if self.lock_time == 0 {
        return true;
    }
    // Per BIP-65: lock_time is ignored if all inputs have sequence=0xffffffff
    if self.inputs_all_sequence_max {
        return true;
    }
    if self.lock_time < LOCKTIME_THRESHOLD {
        self.lock_time <= tip_height
    } else {
        now.duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)
            .map(|d| self.lock_time as u64 <= d.as_secs())
            .unwrap_or(false)
    }
}
// Add field to RebroadcastState:
inputs_all_sequence_max: bool,
// Populate in new():
inputs_all_sequence_max: tx.input.iter().all(|i| i.sequence == 0xffffffff),
// Caveat: requires storing the boolean at enqueue time when the Transaction is still available.
```
</details>

The `WalletInterface::clamp_heights_to(tip)` call returns `()`, so any internal failure (e.g., a failed metadata write to persist the clamped heights) is silently swallowed. Because this runs during startup before the background worker starts, a failure here would leave wallet metadata in an inconsistent state with no diagnostic signal. The trait method should return `StorageResult<()>` (or at minimum `Result<(), impl Error>`), and the call site in `lifecycle.rs` should propagate the error with `?`. If `clamp_heights_to` is intentionally infallible (purely in-memory), document that constraint explicitly on the trait method to prevent future implementors from silently discarding errors.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// WalletInterface trait
async fn clamp_heights_to(&mut self, tip: u32) -> StorageResult<()>;

// lifecycle.rs
if let Some(tip) = storage.block_headers().read().await.get_tip_height().await {
    wallet.write().await.clamp_heights_to(tip).await?;
}
```
</details>

`rotated_quorums_per_cycle` is keyed by the cycle-boundary block hash; `truncate_above` retains an entry if that hash maps to height ≤ fork_height. A cycle that *started* before the fork but whose DKG commitment block was mined in the now-orphaned branch (height > fork_height) therefore survives truncation, retaining public keys from the orphaned quorum. The `masternode_ready = false` hard-block prevents immediate misuse, but correctness then relies entirely on `feed_qr_info` overwriting the stale entry before validation resumes. For cycles outside the QRInfo depth window the stale entry persists indefinitely, and any code path that reads `rotated_quorums_per_cycle` after `masternode_ready` flips true will use orphaned keys, risking false-positive ChainLock acceptance. The fix is to key or filter by commitment-block height rather than cycle-start height, or explicitly clear cycles whose commitment block is not confirmed on the new chain.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// Option A: during truncate_above, also drop any rotated quorum entry whose
// commitment height is above the fork. Requires tracking commitment height per entry.

// Option B (conservative, no schema change): after truncate_above, clear *all*
// rotated_quorums_per_cycle entries for cycles whose cycle-start height is within
// WORK_DIFF_DEPTH of fork_height, since their commitment block may have been orphaned.
self.rotated_quorums_per_cycle.retain(|cycle_hash, _| {
    self.block_container
        .get_height(cycle_hash)
        .is_some_and(|h| h + WORK_DIFF_DEPTH <= height)
});
```
</details>

When `send_qrinfo_for_tip` fails, the new code sets the manager state to `WaitForEvents` and then returns the `Err` to the caller (`handle_sync_event`). If the sync-coordinator framework performs additional state management on `SyncResult::Err` returns from `handle_sync_event` (e.g. logging a fatal condition or resetting the manager), the `WaitForEvents` state set here would be overwritten and the manager's recovery path (waiting for a future `BlockHeaderSyncComplete` event) would be silently invalidated. Returning `Ok(vec![])` after setting the recovery state avoids this inconsistency: the state machine recovers naturally through its tick handler or the next sync event, which is preferable for a transient network failure.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
let result = self.send_qrinfo_for_tip(requests).await;
if result.is_err() {
    tracing::warn!("send_qrinfo_for_tip failed during rewind, awaiting reconnect");
    self.set_state(SyncState::WaitForEvents);
    // Return Ok so the framework does not treat this as a fatal error;
    // the tick handler will retry once the state allows it.
    return Ok(vec![]);
}
result
```
</details>

When the generation check fires (`req_gen != current_gen`), the code returns early before calling `pipeline.receive_block(block)`. That function is the only caller of `coordinator.receive(hash)`, which removes the hash from `in_flight` and `generations`. The stale hash therefore occupies a concurrency slot in the pipeline until either the timeout fires or the `ChainReorg` event arrives and replaces the pipeline entirely. In practice the `ChainReorg` event follows the generation bump in the same coordinator cycle, making this self-correcting, but the window is real and grows with latency between the cascade and event delivery. The cleanest fix is to expose a `pipeline.cancel_in_flight(hash)` helper and call it before returning, so the slot is freed immediately regardless of when the event propagates.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// In BlocksPipeline, add:
pub(super) fn cancel_in_flight(&mut self, hash: &BlockHash) {
    if self.coordinator.receive(hash) { /* slot freed */ }
}

// In sync_manager.rs, replace the early-return block with:
if req_gen != current_gen {
    tracing::debug!("dropping stale Block {}: generation {} != {}", ...);
    self.pipeline.cancel_in_flight(hashed_block.hash());
    return Ok(vec![]);
}
```
</details>

`tip_hash` calls `.expect("ForkCandidate must carry at least one header")`, which will abort the process rather than return an error if the invariant is violated. In a library context the invariant may hold today, but a future bug that allows an empty candidate to escape the fork buffer will produce an opaque crash instead of a `SyncError`. Returning `Option<BlockHash>` or `Result<BlockHash, SyncError>` lets callers decide how to handle the violation and keeps the error visible in logs. The `drive_reorg` callsite already uses `?` propagation, so threading the result type through is low-cost.

```suggestion
pub(crate) fn tip_hash(&self) -> Option<BlockHash> {
    self.headers.last().map(|h| *h.hash())
}
```

`pool.remove_peer(&victim)` removes the victim from the logical pool, but the reader task spawned for that peer holds its own `Arc<RwLock<Peer>>` and keeps reading from the open TCP socket indefinitely — until a TCP error or shutdown. During that window the evicted peer's messages continue being dispatched through `MessageDispatcher`, its score is decaying, and its heap allocation is retained. This is new surface introduced by this PR: eviction is a novel removal path that, unlike the reader's own exit path, does not cause the reader to terminate. A minimal fix is to store per-peer `CancellationToken` objects so eviction can signal the reader to exit; alternatively, mark the peer as 'evicted' in `Peer` and have the reader check that flag at the top of its loop.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// In start_peer_reader, propagate a per-peer token:
let peer_token = CancellationToken::new();
// Store token in a map keyed by addr (e.g. Arc<Mutex<HashMap<SocketAddr, CancellationToken>>>)
// In try_evict_for_incoming, after pool.remove_peer:
if let Some(token) = self.peer_tokens.lock().await.remove(&victim) {
    token.cancel();
}
```
</details>

`build_locator` fetches each locator entry via individual `storage.get_header(height).await?` calls inside a loop. For a 10K-block chain the locator is ≤32 entries so the absolute cost is small, but the method holds the storage read-lock across all of them and dispatches each as a separate async call. A `load_headers` batch call for the computed heights would reduce round-trips and simplify the method. This is a cleanness concern rather than a correctness issue.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

````suggestion
Compute the full set of heights first, then issue one batch read:
```rust
let heights = compute_locator_heights(tip_height); // pure function
let storage = self.header_storage.read().await;
let mut locator = Vec::with_capacity(heights.len());
for h in heights {
    if let Some(header) = storage.get_header(h).await? {
        locator.push(header.block_hash());
    }
}
```
Note: `compute_locator_heights` is a small pure helper that applies the dashd step-doubling algorithm to produce the height list; it has no async surface.
````
</details>

Both `handle_headers_pipeline` and the tick handler now always pass `&[]` to `send_pending` during sync, relying on the invariant that the segment tip is ahead of storage at those call sites. This is correct today because `receive_headers` always advances the segment before `send_pending` is called, making the full-locator branch in `send_pending` unreachable via the sync path. However, the function signature still advertises a `tip_locator` parameter whose full-locator path is now dead for the dominant codepath (sync), while remaining live only in the post-sync announcement reset path. If the call order changes — e.g., `store_headers` moved before `send_pending` so storage catches up to the segment mid-batch — the invariant silently breaks: storage and segment tip would agree, `tip_locator.first() == Some(&segment.current_tip_hash)` would evaluate true with the full locator but is given `&[]` so the condition is never checked, and the fork-detection locator goes unsent. Consider splitting the callsite intent: use an enum or bool parameter (`use_full_locator: bool`) so the caller explicitly opts into the single-entry mode, or document the invariant in `send_pending`'s signature as a `debug_assert!(tip_locator.is_empty() || tip_locator[0] == ...)`.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// In send_pending, add a guard so the invariant is machine-checked:
pub fn send_pending(
    &mut self,
    requests: &RequestSender,
    tip_locator: &[BlockHash],
) -> SyncResult<usize> {
    // Callers that pass a non-empty locator must anchor it at the tip segment's
    // current hash; otherwise the locator is silently ignored and a single-entry
    // fallback is used, defeating the purpose of supplying it.
    debug_assert!(
        tip_locator.is_empty()
            || self
                .segments
                .iter()
                .find(|s| s.target_height.is_none())
                .is_none_or(|s| tip_locator.first() == Some(&s.current_tip_hash)),
        "tip_locator[0] must match tip segment current_tip_hash"
    );
    // ... rest unchanged
}
```
</details>

`InvalidArgument` is used for two semantically different situations: `truncate_above(target)` where `target < start_height` (a genuine bad-argument call-site error) and `get_items` when the requested range spans a segment queued for deletion (a data-availability failure caused by prior truncation, not a bad argument). Callers that match on `StorageError::InvalidArgument` cannot distinguish 'programmer called this wrong' from 'this data was legitimately truncated'. As Phase 2/3 layers start catching and reacting to these errors, the ambiguity will force every caller to inspect the error message string to figure out which case they're in — a brittle design. A dedicated variant like `StorageError::DataTruncated` or `StorageError::NotAvailable` for the range-read case would keep the invariant-violation path (`InvalidArgument`) distinct from the state-driven path.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// In error.rs, add alongside InvalidArgument:
#[error("Data not available (truncated): {0}")]
DataTruncated(String),

// In get_items, use the new variant:
return Err(StorageError::DataTruncated(format!(
    "get_items range {:?} extends into segment {} queued for deletion",
    height_range, segment_id
)));
```
</details>

In `rebuild_utxos`, records are sorted by `context.block_info().map(|info| info.height())`. Because `None < Some(x)` in Rust's `Option` ordering, every record demoted to `Mempool` during the rewind (no block height → `None`) is processed before every still-confirmed record. Consider a chain where Tx_A was confirmed at height 11 and Tx_B (spending A:0) was confirmed at height 12; after rewinding to height 10 both become `Mempool` (height = `None`). If Tx_B's txid sorts before Tx_A's, the rebuild processes Tx_B first: `spent_outpoints.insert(A:0)` runs, but `utxos.remove(A:0)` is a no-op because A:0 hasn't been inserted yet. Tx_A is then processed second and unconditionally inserts A:0 into `utxos`, leaving a spent outpoint visible as a spendable UTXO. This inflates the balance and will cause double-spend failures if the wallet tries to spend A:0. The same issue affects cross-mempool chains where two formerly-confirmed transactions both demote to `Mempool`. The fix is to guard every UTXO insert with a `spent_outpoints` check — the identical pattern already used in `update_utxos` via `is_outpoint_spent`. Caveat: within a purely-mempool chain (two `None`-height records) a simple guard is sufficient; topological sorting would also be correct but is overkill given the guard.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

````suggestion
In the `rebuild_utxos` inner output loop, add the same guard already present in `update_utxos`:

```rust
let outpoint = OutPoint { txid, vout: vout as u32 };
if self.spent_outpoints.contains(&outpoint) {
    // Already spent by a previously-processed record in this rebuild.
    continue;
}
let txout = dashcore::TxOut { ... };
let mut utxo = Utxo::new(...);
// ... set flags ...
self.utxos.insert(outpoint, utxo);
```

This makes the result order-independent: whichever record is processed first, if its spending transaction's input already inserted the predecessor into `spent_outpoints`, the UTXO will be skipped when the funding transaction is later processed.
````
</details>

When `fh_tip_after` is `None` (no filter headers at all), `fh_bound` is set to `0` via `unwrap_or(0)`. If the filter storage has `start_height > 0`—as it would for any SPV client that began syncing from a checkpoint at some non-zero height—`SegmentCache::truncate_above(0)` returns `StorageError::InvalidArgument` because `target_height (0) < start_height (N)`. This error propagates through `check_and_repair_consistency` and aborts startup entirely. Even when `start_height == 0`, truncating to 0 leaves a filter entry at height 0 with no corresponding filter header, silently violating the invariant being enforced. The correct fix is to treat a `None` fh_tip separately rather than mapping it to 0.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

````suggestion
Replace the `unwrap_or(0)` pattern with an explicit match so the absent-filter-headers case is handled without triggering an invalid truncation:
```rust
// Filter invariant: `filter_tip <= filter_header_tip`.
let fh_tip_after = filter_headers.read().await.get_filter_tip_height().await?;
let f_tip = filters.read().await.filter_tip_height().await?;
if let Some(fh_bound) = fh_tip_after {
    if f_tip > fh_bound {
        tracing::warn!(
            "consistency: filter tip {} > filter header tip {}, truncating",
            f_tip, fh_bound
        );
        let mut guard = filters.write().await;
        guard.truncate_above(fh_bound).await?;
        guard.persist(storage_path).await?;
    }
} else if f_tip > 0 {
    // No filter headers exist but filters do. We cannot call truncate_above
    // below start_height, so log a warning; the BIP157 chain-integrity check
    // below will not run (no start height), but the next full sync will
    // overwrite the orphaned filters.
    tracing::warn!(
        "consistency: filters exist (tip {}) but no filter headers present",
        f_tip
    );
}
```
Caveat: the `f_tip > 0` branch cannot fully clear filter storage when `start_height > 0`; a dedicated `clear_all` API would be needed for complete recovery in that edge case.
````
</details>

`clear_all` collects segment IDs only from `self.segments` and `self.evicted`. After `load_or_new`, only the first and last segments are paged into memory; all intermediate segments stay on disk. Those IDs are never added to `self.to_delete`, so the subsequent `persist()` call (in `check_and_repair_consistency`) removes only the two boundary files and leaves every intermediate segment untouched. On the next restart, `load_or_new` rediscovers the surviving files, `tip_height` is restored to the pre-clear value, and the consistency check emits the same warning and runs the same incomplete repair indefinitely. For a mainnet filter store at height ~1.5M (≈30 segments of 50 k each), only segments 0 and 29 are deleted; segments 1–28 survive. The fix is to derive the full segment ID range from `start_height`/`tip_height` so every file that could hold data is queued; `persist()` already tolerates `NotFound` silently for IDs that have no backing file.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
pub fn clear_all(&mut self) {
    if let (Some(start), Some(tip)) = (self.start_height, self.tip_height) {
        let start_seg = Self::height_to_segment_id(start);
        let end_seg   = Self::height_to_segment_id(tip);
        for id in start_seg..=end_seg {
            self.segments.remove(&id);
            self.evicted.remove(&id);
            self.to_delete.insert(id);
        }
    } else {
        // Nothing on disk; clear whatever is in memory.
        let ids: Vec<u32> = self.segments.keys()
            .chain(self.evicted.keys())
            .copied()
            .collect();
        for id in ids {
            self.segments.remove(&id);
            self.evicted.remove(&id);
            self.to_delete.insert(id);
        }
    }
    self.tip_height  = None;
    self.start_height = None;
}
```
</details>

The `force` flag in `handle_reorg` / `run_guards_and_cascade` only bypasses the depth cap (`if !force { depth_check }`). The chainlock floor guard — `is_below_chainlock_floor(candidate.ancestor_height, cl_height)` — runs unconditionally regardless of `force`. In an eclipse-attack recovery scenario a node accumulates fake chainlocks up to height K; a legitimate CLSig then arrives at height H > K pointing to a different chain whose common ancestor A < K. `drive_reorg(candidate, true)` fires, but the chainlock floor guard rejects the candidate and adds its tip hash to the deny-list with TTL = K. Because the node is stuck, `best_chainlock_height` never advances past K, the deny-list entry never evicts, and the node cannot recover. The PR description says 'chainlock floor still applies' but this directly contradicts the forced-reorg use-case of escaping an eclipse attack.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

````suggestion
Add a `force` gate to the chainlock floor branch analogous to the depth-cap gate:
```rust
if !force {
    match best_chainlock_height {
        Some(cl_height) => {
            if is_below_chainlock_floor(candidate.ancestor_height, cl_height) {
                // reject ...
            }
        }
        None => { /* fresh-client floor */ }
    }
}
```
Caveat: bypassing the chainlock floor with `force` means a forged CLSig with a valid BLS signature (requiring quorum compromise) could rewrite finalized blocks. The depth-cap bypass already makes that assumption; allowing the floor to be bypassed is consistent but should be documented explicitly. The deny-list and single-flight guards still provide some backstop.
````
</details>

In `selection_weights`, the formula `max(1, score + REPUTATION_WEIGHT_OFFSET)` maps score directly to weight: score -50 (best peer) → weight 1; score 0 (neutral) → weight 51; score 99 (near-ban) → weight 150. This gives the worst non-banned peer a 150× selection advantage over the most well-behaved peer, and 3× over a neutral one. The tests `test_next_peer_weighted_selection_distribution` and `test_selection_weights_match_score_offset` explicitly verify and lock in this inverted behavior. An adversary who deliberately accumulates misbehavior just below the ban threshold (e.g., by sending invalid messages) maximizes their selection weight without being banned, receiving far more request traffic than trusted peers. The fix is to invert the mapping so lower scores yield higher weights. Note that all three weight-related tests will need their assertions updated to reflect the corrected direction.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// Replace:
(rep.score + REPUTATION_WEIGHT_OFFSET).max(1) as u32
// With:
(MAX_MISBEHAVIOR_SCORE - rep.score).max(1) as u32
// score -50 → weight 150 (selected most)
// score  0 → weight 100
// score 99 → weight 1 (selected least)
```
</details>

The previous `WeightedIndex` error fallback (`rng.gen_range(0..peers.len())`) silently selected a banned peer; the replacement `unreachable!` now panics the process. `next_peer` is called with the raw, un-filtered pool in two paths: (1) when `capable.is_empty() && !required` falls through to `self.next_peer(&peers)`, and (2) in the outer `else` branch when no service flag is specified. In either path, if all peers in the pool are banned (e.g. three peers each sent two invalid headers ≥ 50 pts each in a short window), `selection_weights` returns all-zero weights, `WeightedIndex::new` returns `Err`, and the process crashes. An adversarial Sybil cluster that deliberately triggers bans in all pool slots can reliably crash the SPV client. Fix by making `next_peer` return `Option<…>` and having callers return an error rather than panicking; alternatively, call `filter_unbanned` on `peers` before every invocation of `next_peer`.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
// In next_peer, replace the unreachable! arm:
Err(_) => {
    // All peers are currently banned; bubble up as an error instead of crashing.
    return (peers[0].0, peers[0].1.clone()); // or make next_peer return Option/Result
}

// Better: change next_peer signature to Option<(SocketAddr, Arc<RwLock<Peer>>)>
// and return None when WeightedIndex fails, letting callers handle
// the no-eligible-peer case with a proper NetworkError.
```
</details>

When `truncate_above` removes a segment from memory and queues it in `to_delete` (lines 362-366), the corresponding disk file is NOT deleted until the next `persist()`. If new items are then stored at heights belonging to that dropped segment before `persist()` runs, `get_segment_mut` (line 165) does not check `to_delete` before calling `Segment::load`. The old stale file is loaded from disk, restoring all pre-truncation items. The subsequent `Segment::insert` call then hits `debug_assert!(self.items[offset] == I::sentinel())` — a guaranteed panic in debug builds, or silent data corruption in release builds where old and new items coexist in the same segment. This is the exact scenario triggered by a reorg: `truncate_above` is called, then new headers are immediately re-synced and stored into the truncated range.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

````suggestion
In `get_segment_mut`, add a check for `to_delete` before loading from disk:

```rust
let segment = if let Some(segment) = self.evicted.remove(segment_id) {
    segment
} else if self.to_delete.remove(segment_id) {
    // Segment queued for deletion — don't load the stale file.
    // Removing from to_delete is safe: atomic_write will overwrite
    // any old file on persist, so explicit pre-deletion is not required.
    Segment::new(*segment_id, vec![], SegmentState::Dirty)
} else {
    Segment::load(&self.segments_dir, *segment_id).await?
};
```

Note: `Segment::new` with an empty `Vec` resizes to all sentinels, satisfying `Segment::insert`'s debug-assert. Removing from `to_delete` is safe because `Segment::persist` uses `atomic_write` which replaces the entire file, eliminating any old data. If you prefer the file to be explicitly deleted before the new write, keep the `to_delete.insert` and only skip the disk load.
````
</details>

`test_input_conflict_drops_pending_rebroadcast` calls `evict_conflicting_rebroadcasts` directly, bypassing `drain_wallet_events`. The actual production path goes through the `WalletEvent::BlockProcessed` arm of `drain_wallet_events` — a bug in that arm (wrong variant matched, wrong fields destructured) would go undetected. A test should fire a `WalletEvent::BlockProcessed` event on the channel, call `drain_wallet_events`, and then assert the conflicting entry is evicted.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
async fn test_input_conflict_via_channel_evicts_rebroadcast() {
    let (mut manager, wallet, _requests, _rx) = manager_with_wallet_events().await;
    // ... enqueue a demoted tx as in the existing test ...
    manager.drain_wallet_events().await;
    assert!(manager.pending_rebroadcast.contains_key(&demoted_txid));

    // Fire BlockProcessed through the channel instead of calling evict_conflicting_rebroadcasts directly
    let _ = wallet.read().await.event_sender().send(WalletEvent::BlockProcessed {
