Each loop iteration rebuilds `released_outpoints` by scanning every txid in `already_demoted` — a set that grows by `new_demoted` each round. On the Kth iteration the algorithm does O(K × |accounts|) work to collect outpoints, even though the only newly reachable descendants must spend outputs of the K-1th wave. The cascade is correct but asymptotically O(D²) in the depth of the spend chain times accounts, rather than O(D) with a frontier approach. For typical user wallets this rarely matters, but a wallet that has been used for many chained payments can hit quadratic blowup during a deep reorg. Fix: track a `frontier` of only the txids demoted in the previous round and limit `released_outpoints` collection to that frontier.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
let mut frontier: Vec<Txid> = demoted_txids.clone();
loop {
    // Only collect outpoints released by the *previous* wave.
    let released_outpoints: BTreeSet<OutPoint> = {
        let mut set = BTreeSet::new();
        for account in self.accounts.all_accounts() {
            for txid in &frontier {
                if let Some(record) = account.transactions().get(txid) {
                    for vout in 0..record.transaction.output.len() as u32 {
                        set.insert(OutPoint { txid: *txid, vout });
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        set
    };
    if released_outpoints.is_empty() { break; }
    // ... rest of candidate scan unchanged
    frontier = new_demoted;
    // Note: this still requires tracking already_demoted to avoid re-demoting.
}
```
</details>

