The new `has_owned_input` check uses `self.spent_outpoints.contains(&input.previous_output)`. `spent_outpoints` accumulates the *inputs* of already-processed records, so this detects a double-spend situation (two records trying to consume the same outpoint), not whether the current transaction's input is our own UTXO. With the current sort order (`None < Some(height)`, so mempool records sort first), a mempool child spending a block parent's output is processed before the parent. At that point neither `spent_outpoints` nor `utxos` contains the parent's output, so `has_owned_input` is false and `is_trusted` remains false for the change output. Balance after rewind still mis-categorises trusted change as `unconfirmed` instead of `confirmed`. To fix correctly, either (a) reverse the sort so block records are processed before mempool ones and use `self.utxos.contains_key(&input.previous_output)`, or (b) collect all wallet-owned outpoints in a pre-pass before the rebuild loop, then use that set for the `has_owned_input` test. The `spent_outpoints.contains(&outpoint)` guard added in the same diff (which prevents resurrecting spent UTXOs) is correct and resolves the prior sort-order blocker; only the `has_owned_input` signal is wrong.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```suggestion
