The doc comment states "highest height `h` such that every header in `[start, h]` chains correctly via `prev_blockhash` to its parent in `header_hash_index`", implying the complete chain from `start` to `h` is validated. The implementation walks backward from the tip and returns at the *first* height where `header_hash_index[prev_blockhash_of(height)] == height - 1`—a single-link check. A chain with a corrupt segment in the middle (heights 0–2 intact, height 3 missing, heights 4–tip valid with all links in the hash index) would cause the function to return `tip` even though the range `[start, tip]` is not fully connected. For the intended crash-recovery scenario, where corruption is always at the highest written headers, this works correctly, but future callers may rely on the stronger guarantee implied by the docstring.

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

````suggestion
Correct the docstring to match what is actually implemented:
```rust
/// Highest height `h` for which the immediate parent link
/// (`header_hash_index[prev_blockhash_of(h)] == h - 1`) is found in the
/// index and the header at `h` is present in storage. Walks backward from
/// the current tip, returning the first height that satisfies these two
/// conditions. Returns `None` when the storage is empty. Mid-chain gaps
/// are not detected; callers must not assume that `[start, result]` is
/// fully contiguous.
```
````
</details>

